.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-SET-QUEUE-VISIBILITY 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-set-queue-visibility \- set a queue's default visibility timeout
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eqs set-queue-visibility
.BR \-\-queue | \-q
.I ERN
.BR \-\-visibility | \-v
.I SECONDS
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets a queue's default visibility timeout: how long a message received
from it stays invisible to other consumers before it becomes available
again.
.PP
This is the figure every receive starts from, and the queue-level
counterpart to
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-set-message-visibility (1),
which changes the timeout of one message that has already been received.
A queue created without one gets 30 seconds.
.PP
The timeout is what makes a receive a lease rather than a handover: a
consumer that takes a message and then dies has not lost it, because the
message reappears once its window expires and another consumer gets it.
Sizing it is therefore a judgement about the slowest handler you are
willing to wait for \- too short and a message is redelivered while it is
still being processed, too long and a crashed consumer's messages sit
idle until the window runs out.
.SH IN-FLIGHT MESSAGES ARE NOT CHANGED
Only the default changes. Messages already in flight keep the window
they were given when they were received, and the new value applies from
the next receive onwards.
.PP
That is deliberate in both directions: shortening the timeout here would
make a consumer's lease expire under it while it is still working, and
lengthening it would hold back a message whose consumer has already
given up on it. To change one message that is currently in flight, use
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-set-message-visibility (1)
instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fIERN\fR
ERN of the queue to change. Required. Resolve a name to its ERN with
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-visibility " " \fISECONDS\fR
The new default, between 0 and 43200 seconds (12 hours) \- the same
range AWS SQS allows, and the same range one message may be given, so a
queue default can never be a figure no message could actually take.
Required.
.PP
Zero is legal and means a received message becomes available again
immediately, which is occasionally what a fan-out test wants and almost
never what a consumer does.
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I ern
The queue that was changed.
.TP
.I visibility
The default now in force, in seconds.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The timeout was changed.
.TP
.B 1
The queue ERN is missing or names no queue, the value is outside 0\-43200,
the request could not be sent, or the server rejected it. Details are
printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Give a queue whose handler does slow work a five-minute lease:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs set-queue-visibility \e
    --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:onix-queue \e
    --visibility 300
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look the ERN up first if you only know the name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs get-queue-ern --name onix-queue
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what a queue currently uses:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs get-queue-metadata \e
    --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:onix-queue
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
